# Copy to .env.local and fill in with `supabase status` (run from the repo
# root, with the local stack up via `supabase start`).
#
# Local values map from `supabase status` like this (the local CLI still
# prints the LEGACY key names; they fill the same slots):
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL              <- API_URL
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY  <- ANON_KEY
#   SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY                   <- SERVICE_ROLE_KEY
#
# On a hosted Supabase project, the same three come from
# Project Settings -> API. Never commit .env.local — it is gitignored.

# Public: shipped to the browser. Used only for staff sign-in on /login and
# the anon Realtime subscription to live_sessions (lib/db/browser.ts).
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=http://127.0.0.1:54321
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEY=replace-with-publishable-key (sb_publishable_..., or the legacy anon key)

# Server-only. Bypasses RLS — this is the entire data path for the app
# (lib/db/server.ts, serviceClient()). Never expose to the browser, never
# prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.
SUPABASE_SECRET_KEY=replace-with-secret-key (sb_secret_..., or the legacy service_role key)

# Used by scripts/bootstrap-admin.ts to create the first staff login.
# Also readable as CLI args: pnpm bootstrap:admin -- --email=... --password=...
ADMIN_EMAIL=admin@example.com
ADMIN_PASSWORD=replace-with-a-real-password

# Deploy-only (D8/D10). A stable value shared across instances so Server
# Actions with closed-over variables keep working on a multi-instance or
# self-hosted deploy. Not needed for local dev against a single `next dev`
# process. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# NEXT_SERVER_ACTIONS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
